{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790307463,"nonce":"bvrt6V74LzYEHxRpb1Fbq0mJyko=","title":"C SDK header zrtl.h still describes the 4-byte string layout","message":"Strings now carry a 16-byte header (u32 byte_len, u32 char_len, u32 aux, u32 flags; data at +16, 16-aligned) since 4c8ddc90. sdk/zrtl.h still documents [i32 length][bytes] and its macros ZRTL_STRING_DATA, ZRTL_STRING_DATA_MUT, ZRTL_STRING_ALLOC_SIZE and the constructors zrtl_string_new / zrtl_string_from_bytes read and write that layout. A C plugin built against it reads data at +4 (garbage: char_len and aux words), and a string it constructs has no MAGIC/flags word, so string_free and every SDK reader misinterpret it. The header needs the new layout: the four header words, flags (TEXT, IMMORTAL, BUILDER, INDEXED, BOXED_PREFIX, MAGIC), data at +16, and constructors that set char_len/flags (or call into the host's constructors rather than hand-building headers).","files":null}]}